{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md && cat AGENTS.md && cat START_HERE.md'","aggregated_output":"# Make a game on Pavilion\n\nFor an agent asked to build a game on this engine. Read this page whole (about 3k tokens); it\nis the only one you need to start. Search the others when a step points to them. The engine\nwas made for agents: everything runs, renders and checks headless, and every tool is a CLI\ncommand, a REPL line and an MCP tool.\n\n## What you get\n- **A simulation** on the CPU at 60 ticks a second (`pav_core`): characters that walk, jump,\n  climb and swim, rapier physics, pathfinding, projectiles, zones, procedural animation,\n  props from a library. The same inputs and seed always play the same: snapshots, rewind\n  and replays are exact.\n- **A WebGPU renderer** (`pav_render`, `pav_view`): lighting, shadows, bloom, styles and\n  filters (cel, pixel, painterly...), set per level as data.\n- **Synthesized sound** (`pav_audio`): it plays the simulation's events, no audio files.\n- **Tools** (`pav`): capture a frame, step time, drive the player, run your game's bot over\n  many seeds, record and replay. Images come back as PNGs you can look at.\n- **Three builds** from one source: a window on Linux and Windows, and the browser (also as\n  one self-contained HTML file).\n\nShardfall, the hack-and-slash in `crates/pav_core/src/arpg/`, and the pavilion of demo rooms\nare built on the same engine. They are examples, not starting points: start from the\n**starter game**.\n\n## Read this, skip that\n- **Use:** this page; `crates/pav_core/src/games/` (the hook and the starter, about 600\n  lines); `rooms/_template.toml` (the level format: search it for a field); `docs/REFERENCE.md`\n  \"Tools\" (search for a tool); `pav help`.\n- **`AGENTS.md`** is the rulebook for agents working on the engine and Shardfall. Its\n  \"Key rules\" apply to your game too (below). Its workflow does not: `docs/HISTORY.md`,\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md`, pull requests, escalations and the asset-store plans are the\n  maintainers'. Follow your own task's instructions for commits and delivery.\n- **Skip:** `docs/HISTORY.md`, `docs/ASSET_SYSTEM_PLAN.md`, `docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`,\n  Shardfall's code and data (`arpg/`, `game/`), `pavilion-lite/` (an older, frozen engine).\n  If you need to read engine source to use the engine, say so in your feedback (below).\n\n## Quick start\n```sh\ncargo build -p pav_tools --bin pav                     # first build: a few minutes\nalias pav=./target/debug/pav\npav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=10               # the starter's bot wins 10/10\npav capture scene=starter ticks=120 width=640 height=360 out=out/starter.png\nprintf 'load scene=starter\\ninput move=[1,0] ticks=60\\nplay\\ncapture out=out/a.png\\n' | pav repl\ncargo test -p pav_core --test games                    # the starter's tests\ncargo run -p pav_app -- --scene starter                # the window (WASD; needs a display)\n```\n`START_HERE.md` has the Linux packages to install. Look at captures with your image tool;\nsmall sizes are cheaper. With MCP (`.mcp.json`), the same tools are `mcp__shardfall__*`.\n\n**The edit loop.** Running from the repository's root, `pav` reads your level file at every\n`load` (and `play seeds=`), so changes to the map and to its `[game]` numbers need no rebuild.\nA change to Rust code rebuilds `pav` (about 2 minutes on 4 cores) and a test binary (as long\nagain): batch code edits, and put balance numbers in `[game]`.\n\n## Your game in six steps\n1. **Copy the starter.** `crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs` and `starter.toml` to\n   `<key>.rs` and `<key>.toml` in the same folder. Rename the struct; set `const KEY` to your\n   key (lowercase, no `/`); point `include_str!` at your level.\n2. **Register it.** In `games/mod.rs`: `pub mod <key>;` and one line in `GAMES`:\n   `entry::<mykey::MyGame>(\"One sentence about it.\", mykey::build)`. Now `load scene=<key>`\n   works in the tools, the window (`--scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`).\n3. **Draw the level** in `<key>.toml` (next section), with your rules' numbers in its `[game]`\n   table (the starter's `Rules`). `pav room_check path=<file>` checks it.\n4. **Write the rules** in your `Game` impl (the hook, below).\n5. **Teach the bot** every rule you add (`Game::bot`): it is how you, the tests and the next\n   agent find out whether the game can be won.\n6. **Copy the tests** in `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs` to `tests/<key>.rs`: the bot wins on\n   ten seeds, a careless player loses, and snapshots, rewind and replays are exact.\n\n## The level: a room file\nA level is a room file (`rooms/_template.toml` documents every field; the pavilion's\n`rooms/*.toml` are working examples). Rooms built this way stand alone, with no wing or\ncorridor. In a room file:\n- `[layout]`: an ASCII map per floor (`[[layout.layer]]`, 1 cell = 1 m; column = x east,\n  row = z south) and a `legend` per character: floor and wall `blocks` (heights, colours,\n  `destructible`, `crumble`, `ghost`), ladders, `prop`s, `label`s, `grass`, trigger `zone`s\n  (`kill`, `water`, `checkpoint`, `conveyor`, `bounce`, `pad`, `camera`, course\n  `start`/`gate`/`finish`) and `marker = \"<name>\"`.\n- **Markers** are your own pieces: `crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\")` lists every\n  marked cell (world positions, map order). `marker = \"player\"` is the start.\n- `[[npc]]`: characters (`kind = biped | spider | lizard | beetle | blob | quadruped`, `look` colours and\n  proportions, `ai = idle | wander | patrol | circle | follow | guard`). Your game can drive\n  them instead (the starter's slimes).\n- `[[object]]`: library props (`asset = \"LIB/core/<name>\"`; `pav assets find=barrel` searches\n  the store, `assets shelf=prop` lists the best), physics objects, `[[joint]]`s and `[[chain]]`s.\n- `[camera]` (`tilt`, `distance`, `yaw`, `ortho`), `[view]` (light, sky, fog, bloom, styles,\n  filters: `pav params prefix=view` lists them), `[params]` (movement and physics while in\n  the level: `pav params`).\n\n- `[game]`: your rules' numbers, free-form; your `build` reads them (`def.game`).\n- `entrance` is required by the format; with a `player` marker a level doesn't use it.\n\nRead the level with `games::level_text(KEY, include_str!(...))`: the file on disk when running\nfrom the repository, else the copy built in (tests, the browser, an executable). The camera\nfollows the player; to see a whole level from above, `camera preset=top` then\n`set path=camera.distance value=30` (raise it for bigger levels) and `capture`.\n\n## The hook: `pav_core::games::Game`\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct MyGame { /* the rules' state: score, timers, entity ids, outcome */ }\n\nimpl Game for MyGame {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"mygame\";\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>)\n        -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;          // before movement: inputs for characters you drive\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>); // after physics: rules\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;        // text lines, top left; `play` returns them\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value; // your state for tools and tests\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;                   // Playing | Won | Lost\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;         // what a good player would press now\n}\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()>; // the scene; sets sim.state.play\n```\nEvery method has a default. `build` gets the rest of a scene name `mygame/<rest>` (a level\nnumber, say). Each tick runs: behaviours, your `pre`, characters move (your inputs replace\ntheir own), physics, shots move and hit, your `post`. While `pre`/`post` run, your game is out\nof `sim.state.play`: use `self` for your state and `sim` for the world. In `post`, `events`\nalready holds this tick's events: read them to react (`Hit { target, .. }` names the\ncharacter a shot struck: it is stunned, knocked back and safe from shots for 0.6 s), and push\nyour own. From outside, `sim.play()?.get::<MyGame>()` reads your game; in a test,\n`sim.state.play.as_mut().unwrap().get_mut::<MyGame>().unwrap()` changes it.\n\n## Building blocks\n| You need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| The player | `sim.state.player`; it walks by the keys' `InputFrame` (`move_dir`, `buttons::JUMP`, `PRIMARY`...); `sim.feet(id)` is where a character stands (`pos` is its centre) |\n| Characters | `[[npc]]` in the level, or `sim.spawn_npc(name, feet, facing, PuppetDef::preset(BodyPlan::Blob), None, None)`; drive them from `pre` |\n| Pickups, hazards, scenery | `sim.spawn(Spawn::new(\"name\", pos).visual(Visual::new(shape, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"))).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed }))`; `sim.despawn(id)`; `sim.set_position(id, feet)` |\n| Moving platforms, emitters, spinners | `Behavior::Move`, `Rotate`, `Emitter`, `Spawner` (`entity.rs`), or `[[object]]` in the level |\n| Shots | `sim.state.projectiles.spawn(Projectile { pos, vel, radius, life, color, knockback, gravity, owner, team, damage })`: plain structs, stopped by walls; `Team::Enemy` shots hit characters (a `Hit` event), `Team::Player` shots hit things with `health` |\n| Pathfinding | `nav::NavGrid::for_room(sim)` (the level's walkable grid); `grid.path(from, to)`; `grid.flow(goal, reach)` steers many chasers at once; `grid.walkable(p)` |\n| Randomness | `sim.state.rng` only (`range`, `below`, `f32`): seeded, so a seed always plays the same |\n| Messages | `sim.say(\"text\")`: centre top for a few seconds |\n| Sound and effects | push `SimEvent`s from `pre`/`post`: `Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`, `Shot`, `Jump`, `Land`... The renderer and the synth play them |\n| Tuning | your numbers: the level's `[game]`; the engine's: `pav params`, `pav set path=movement.speed value=4.5` (movement, physics, camera, view), and a level's `[params]` set them while in it |\n| Looks | the level's `[view]`; `pav look` lists looks and filters; `rooms/mix_match.toml` mixes styles per part |\n| Animation | characters animate procedurally; motion clips: `pav assets shelf=clip`, then `clips = [\"SET/Clip\"]` on an `[[npc]]` |\n\n## Rules that keep it working\n- **Same inputs and seed, same game.** Random numbers come from `sim.state.rng`; never read the\n  clock; never let `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (`BTreeMap`, or sort).\n- **All game state lives in your struct or in entities**, and your struct is `Clone` + serde\n  (snapshots are clones; files and the replay hash are its CBOR). A cache that follows from\n  the level (a nav grid) is `#[serde(skip)]` and rebuilt when missing.\n- **`pav_core` has no window, GPU or audio code**, and game code must run in the browser: no\n  threads, blocking calls or file access (embed data with `include_str!`).\n- **Check with the tools, not by reading engine code.** If a tool or error message didn't tell\n  you what you needed, that is engine feedback.\n\n## Check your work (no screen needed)\n| Question | Command (`pav <tool>` or a `pav repl` line) |\n|---|---|\n| Can it be won? Lost? | `play bot=true seeds=10` (wins, seconds, the state of every run not won) |\n| What's on screen? | `capture width=640 height=360 out=out/a.png`; `filmstrip` for motion in one image; to watch the bot, repeat `play bot=true ticks=30` and `capture` in a `pav repl` |\n| Does a rule fire? | `input move=[1,0] ticks=30`, then `play` (HUD, outcome, `status`) |\n| Where is everything? | `entities`, `player`, `status` (with the state hash) |\n| Same every time? | `record_save path=out/r.json`, then `replay path=out/r.json` (checks the hash); `rewind`, `snapshot_save`/`snapshot_load` |\n| Is the level valid? | `room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` |\n| Does it run fast? | `bench scene=<key> ticks=1200` |\n| Does it still pass? | `cargo test -p pav_core --test <key>`, `cargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all` |\n\n## Ship it\n- **Window:** `cargo run -p pav_app -- --scene <key>` (Linux; `xvfb-run -a` without a display).\n  F5 restarts the game (Esc opens the menu).\n- **Windows `.exe`:** `scripts/build-windows.sh` (after `scripts/setup-linux.sh`).\n- **Browser, one file:** `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene <key> --title \"My Game\"\n  --out out/mygame.html`: about 10 MB, runs from `file://` in Chrome or Edge (WebGPU).\n\n## Limits today\n- HUD: text lines only (captures draw them; `ui` reads them). Panels, bars or menus mean egui\n  code in `pav_ui` (draw it from `headless.rs` too, so the tools see it).\n- Drawing from game state without an entity (`pav_view::assets::emit_asset`) and new sounds\n  (`pav_audio`, a closed list) mean code outside `pav_core`.\n- Your game compiles into the engine crate (`pav_core/src/games/`), beside Shardfall.\n- The camera follows the player with the level's `[camera]` settings; there is no\n  per-game camera code. Keep levels about 20 m across, or raise `[camera] distance`.\n- The window's controls card lists the engine demo's keys (bombs, vehicles), not your game's.\n\n## Report back\nWhen you hand the game back, add a short **engine feedback** section, written for an engine\nagent who has never seen your work: what you used, the engine source you had to read and\nwhy, what you wrote that the engine should have had (with the file), wrong or missing docs,\nand the one change that would have helped most. The maintainers log it in\n`docs/FEEDBACK.md`, and what keeps coming back becomes engine work.\n# AGENTS.md — rulebook for coding agents\n\nMaking a new game on the engine? Read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`: the \"Key rules\" below apply to\nyour game, the maintainers' workflow (HISTORY, PROGRESS, PRs, escalations) doesn't.\n\nShardfall is a procedural hack-and-slash game built on the Pavilion engine, written in Rust. The\nprinciples are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, which outranks everything else here. The current state is\n`docs/PROGRESS.md` (one page). Read both before starting; Claude Code loads them, with this file,\nthrough `CLAUDE.md`. The design is in `docs/DESIGN.md` (engine) and `docs/GAME.md` (Shardfall):\nread them when your work touches the design. `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every\ntool family with examples, the controls and what each test suite covers: open the section you\nneed. The journal and past decisions are in `docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it, don't\nread it whole. For a standalone Linux source handoff, `START_HERE.md` covers setup and headless\nCLI examples.\n\n## How the doctrine applies here\nAdopted 2026-10-09, partway through development. Code from before then isn't rewritten to fit;\nfix a mismatch when it gets in the way.\n- **Platform (5, 10):** the development platform is Linux, where agents build and test. The\n  user's Windows `.exe` and the browser build count as part of the target too: a problem the user\n  can see gets fixed now. Replays are promised to match only on the machine that recorded them; to\n  chase something the user saw, start from a snapshot taken just before it.\n- **Renderer (6):** wgpu is the WebGPU implementation: Vulkan on Linux and Windows (DX12 as a\n  Windows option), the browser's own WebGPU on the web, lavapipe where there's no GPU. GPU compute\n  stays cosmetic. The game UI is egui.\n- **API and internals (7, 8):** the game (`pav_core/src/arpg/`) still lives inside the engine\n  crate, so the compiler doesn't enforce the line. The engine modules the game uses are its API:\n  `sim`, `entity`, `frame`, `rng`, `input`, `params`, `statics`, `nav`, `zones`, `character`,\n  `projectile`, `puppet`, `parts`, `moves`, `clips`, `shape`, `color`, `fxdef`, and `games` (the\n  hook a new game implements: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`). Everything else\n  (renderer, physics wiring, synth, importers) is internals. If game work means reading\n  internals, fix the API or the error message.\n- **Versions (9):** current pins stay (Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30, rapier 0.36, egui 0.36, glam 0.33,\n  cpal 0.18). The rule applies to new dependencies and to upgrades.\n- **Assets (4):** no image, model, texture or audio files; fonts are allowed. Animation and\n  objects are readable text: key poses (`anim/*.json`, the format `clips.rs` documents, made with\n  `clip_import`) and props made of named primitive parts (`assets/props`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`).\n  Raw downloads (.glb, .gltf, .obj, .fbx, .bvh, .amc) go to `.cache/` (git-ignored); never commit\n  them, or images. Keep each source's credit and licence. Libraries whose licences forbid sharing\n  what is made from them (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mixamo) are translated only into the git-ignored\n  local tiers (`anim/local`, `assets/props/local`).\n- **Escalation:** ask in chat and log it in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`. If you can schedule a check-in\n  (e.g. `send_later`), set one for 15 minutes and carry on with other work; if not, make the call,\n  log it and continue.\n- **Pavilion Lite** (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen: its renderer runs on the CPU, not WebGPU (6),\n  so it isn't developed further (`docs/ESCALATIONS.md`).\n\n## Working rules\nUnder the doctrine, in priority order:\n1. **Gameplay first, then robustness.**\n2. **Use tools.** If a tool does a job faster, more accurately or cheaper over time, use or build one.\n3. **It must still run well on a modern PC** (the user's RTX 3060 Ti and 4070 Super). Other\n   optimization and compatibility wait (10).\n4. **Approximations are fine if they feel right.** Feel beats physical accuracy.\n5. **Check selectively.** Verify what often breaks or is expensive to debug; skip routine checks\n   of things that almost never break. \"If it's broken, the user will say so\" is valid.\n6. **Cost over speed.** Prefer the CLI/capture tools over reading lots of code.\n\n## Testing budget (prototype stage)\nEvery engine here is a prototype with one user: spend on finding what clearly helps, not on\nmeasuring exactly how much. Completion comes first: a test decides how something is built and is\nrecorded; it never stops, skips or reverts planned work.\n- **Test only to change a decision.** Before a test, name the choice it decides and its cost in\n  US$ as Claude Code bills it, subagents included. If no choice rides on it, or a wrong choice\n  would cost less than the test, skip it. Afterwards, report what it cost.\n- **Size the test to the gap.** Agent runs (benchmarks, judges, eval agents) start small: 3-5 per\n  arm. A clear gap decides it: 2:1 or better in wins, or 25% or more in cost, model calls or\n  errors.\n- **Near 50/50, make the call.** If the first batch is within 60/40 (or 10%), stop: the options\n  are about equally good, so the choice matters little (Fredkin's paradox). Take the simpler,\n  cheaper or more reversible option, log the call and why, and move on. Between clear and close:\n  at most one more small batch, then decide.\n- **Close calls belong to tool tests.** Checks with no model calls (cargo tests, `pav` checks and\n  captures, scripted counts) cost almost nothing per run and repeat exactly, so they may scale up\n  to settle narrow margins. Even then, don't chase gains under 5%; timing differences under 10%\n  are noise.\n- Looks and feel are the expensive, noisy kind: prefer objective proxies; use agent judges only for\n  large, obvious differences, few of them, several items per call; leave fine visual judgement to\n  the user on a quick comparison page.\n- Explore broadly: test distinct hypotheses, not variations of one. Don't add or keep code without\n  a verified benefit.\n- Count cost end to end, in US$, not one tool's traffic: every model call re-reads the agent's\n  whole context.\n- Assets made through the documented loop (a brief, `check` to zero findings, a 4-view sheet at\n  the game camera, at most two fix rounds, `publish` with a review) need no human review: the user\n  accepted 28 of 28 (2026-10-12). Show the user only edge cases, or complex ones you are unsure of.\n- Per PR (from 2026-10-16):\n  - Run the full checks (tests of the touched crates, clippy, fmt, the wasm check when the app\n    builds the change) once, on the head you merge.\n  - After a rebase or a small fix, run only the tests of what changed. A docs-only PR needs none.\n  - Merge as soon as the checks pass; don't wait for a review (Codex has posted none since #45 on\n    2026-10-12). A review that arrives later: fix its real findings in a follow-up PR.\n  - Nothing deploys: there is no hosted site. After a merge that can change the game, build the\n    single-file HTML and send it to the user (`CLAUDE.md`, \"Builds for playing and testing\").\n\n## Architecture\nCrates (the full map, module by module: `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Code map\"):\n- `pav_core`: the simulation, with no window, GPU or audio dependencies: `Sim`, entities, physics\n  (rapier3d), the world, rooms and terrain, characters and puppets, moves and clips, AI and nav,\n  props and materials; the game lives in `src/arpg/`.\n- `pav_render`: the wgpu renderer: shadows, bloom and post effects, the filter stack, procedural\n  meshes and materials, offscreen capture to PNG.\n- `pav_view`: sim frame -> render scene: the camera rig, looks and filters, water, `emit_asset`.\n- `pav_audio`: synthesized sound: cpal output, offline .wav rendering.\n- `pav_tools`: the agent layer: the tool registry, the `pav` CLI and REPL, the MCP server, the\n  live bridge client, and the motion and asset importers.\n- `pav_ui`: Shardfall's egui UI (HUD, inventory, passive tree, touch controls), drawn by the\n  window and, headless, by the tools (`ui`, captures).\n- `pav_app`: the game (`shardfall` binary): window, input, sim thread, live bridge server, the\n  pavilion's egui UI; native and wasm32.\n\nData: `anim/` (moves and motion clips), `rooms/` (room files, embedded in the exe and hot-reloaded;\n`_template.toml` documents every field), `game/` (Shardfall's data), `assets/` (props, materials,\nmodel catalogs), `store/` (the asset store's tables).\nData flow: `Sim::step(InputFrame)` (fixed tick, own thread) -> `Sim::frame()` -> `RenderFrame`\n-> `pav_view::ViewBuilder::build` (interpolates prev/curr) -> `pav_render::Scene` -> `Renderer`.\n\nKey rules:\n- `pav_core` must never depend on graphics, windowing or audio crates.\n- Everything that changes during play lives in `SimState` (cloning it = snapshot). Keep it `Clone`.\n- Same inputs and seed, same state (doctrine 5): randomness comes from `rng`, seeded (the sim's\n  `Rng`, or its hash for seed-derived content), never from the clock; don't let\n  `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (use a `BTreeMap` or sort).\n- Gameplay code reads plain struct fields; expose tunables via `impl Tunable` (see `params.rs`).\n  Tunables are automatically in the tuning panel, the `params`/`set` tools and preset files.\n- Colors in data are sRGB hex (`Color::hex(\"#e8704a\")`); internally linear.\n- Physics precision switch: in `crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml` change `package = \"rapier3d\"` to\n  `\"rapier3d-f64\"`.\n\n## Build, run, test\n```sh\nscripts/setup-linux.sh             # once per machine (apt packages, lavapipe, mingw, wine)\ncargo build                        # everything (Linux)\ncargo test                         # every crate's tests (`-p pav_core` for one crate)\ncargo fmt --all --check            # formatting\ncargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings  # lints, for each crate you touched\ncargo run -p pav_app               # the game (needs a display; Xvfb works with lavapipe)\nscripts/build-windows.sh           # -> target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe\nscripts/smoke-windows.sh 25        # run the .exe under Wine+lavapipe, screenshot + log in out/smoke/\nscripts/smoke-linux.sh 10          # same for the native Linux build\nscripts/build-web.sh               # browser build -> target/web/ (serve over HTTP; Chrome/Edge)\nscripts/build-web.sh --single-file # -> target/web/shardfall.html: one ~10 MB page, runs from file://\nscripts/check-wgsl.sh              # after any WGSL change: Chromium's Tint compiles every module\n```\n`--single-file` packs web/index.html, the JS glue and the gzipped wasm into one page with no\nother loads (`scripts/pack_single_html.py`; `--title --description --theme-color --scene NAME --out PATH`).\nBrowser build: `cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")` code lives in `pav_app` (no threads, async GPU\nsetup); other crates only skip disk access there (the browser has no files) or pick its GPU\nbackend. Check it with `cargo clippy -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`. Headless\nChromium runs it only with the right flags (WebGPU on SwiftShader); those and phone emulation are\nin `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Browser and phones\".\nDisk: cloud containers have a fixed allowance. Dev builds use line-table debug info and no\nincremental cache; if the disk fills up anyway, delete stale binaries in `target/debug/deps`\n(or run `cargo clean`).\n\n## Agent CLI (`pav`) and MCP\nEvery tool works headless (captures use lavapipe when there is no GPU). The same tools are an\nMCP server: `.mcp.json` registers `pav mcp` (stdio), so Claude Code agents in this repo get\nthem as `mcp__shardfall__*` tools (captures come back as images). `pav help` lists every tool\nand its arguments; `docs/REFERENCE.md` (\"Tools\") shows each family with examples.\n```sh\ncargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\npav bench ticks=1200                          # ticks/sec\npav capture scene=test ticks=300 out=out/a.png\nprintf 'step ticks=200\\ncamera preset=top\\ncapture out=out/b.png\\n' | pav repl\nprintf 'input move=[1,0] ticks=30\\ninput press=jump move=[0,1] ticks=40\\nplayer\\n' | pav repl\n```\n- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first. `pav repl`\n  keeps one session across lines (`tool key=value ...` or JSON lines\n  `{\"tool\":\"name\",\"args\":{...}}`; `#` = comment).\n- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n  `filmstrip` tiles N frames into one PNG: the cheapest way to check motion and animation.\n- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n  use them for bug reports.\n- Live bridge: start the game with `--bridge` (or `bridge = \"127.0.0.1:7878\"` in shardfall.toml),\n  then `pav live` is a REPL into the running game and `pav mcp --live` an MCP server for it. Same\n  tools; they act on what is on screen (captures render the game's camera on a second device).\n- Studios edit on the live bridge, each with its own doc: Animation (`shardfall\n  --animation-studio`, `.mcp.animation-studio.json`, `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`), Object\n  (`--asset-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`;\n  materials `docs/MATERIALS.md`) and Creature (`--creature-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`,\n  `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`); `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, `asset-studio.sh` and\n  `creature-studio.sh` build and open each. Pass the last reply's `revision` as `if_revision`,\n  and copy a source clip or asset before changing it.\n- Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries: embedded (BUILTIN, LIB/core) or a\n  shipped pack's (`props::store::SHIPPED`: the KayKit figures). Game code draws library assets\n  from its own state with `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`, \"Library\n  assets in game code\").\n- `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge pin: keep the pin and source hashes\n  intact; don't resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n- Shardfall content: game data lives in `game/*.toml` (`game_reload` re-reads it live). Check a\n  change with the tools: `see` (a screenshot with numbered marks and a legend), `levelmap`,\n  `turntable`, `animsheet`, and `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` (the bot plays down through the\n  levels and reports each; run it after data changes).\n\nAsset store (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): before making a prop or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the best entries and `assets find=words` (`kind=clip` for motion) searches; a miss gives `none` and a route.\nIn the Workshop wing, `library_hall` shows every standard object (generated: after publishing to core, rebuild and run `pav assets reindex=store` and `pav assets reindex=hall`), `limits` what the checks catch and miss, `catalogue` pages through every store row, `store_showcase` shows the materials; the animation wing's `clip_browser` plays every clip.\n\nTools: `scenes load step status entities npcs params set camera capture bench gpu player input\nspawn despawn teleport rewind snapshot_save snapshot_load record_save replay rooms room goto\nroom_reset room_check room_reload stream signal filmstrip camera_bench course feel audio_capture`;\nlooks `look`; teaching `guide`; games on the hook `play`; Shardfall `game hero monster autoplay skills game_reload\nloot_roll give inventory game_cmd ui tree tree_map genome bestiary boss turntable animsheet level\nlevelmap go goto_feature see campaign theme_swatch`; animation `clips clip_import mocap\nanim_reload anim_edit anim_preview`; objects `assets asset_edit asset_preview asset_spawn\nasset_import studio_status`; creatures `creature_catalog creature_edit creature_preview\ncreature_status`.\n\n### Adding a tool\n1. Write `fn t_name(s: &mut Session, a: &Args) -> Result<Output>` in the tool family's module\n   (`asset_tools.rs`, `game_tools.rs`, ...); it rejects unknown arguments, naming the nearest.\n2. Add a `Tool { .. }` entry to `TOOLS` in `tools.rs` (help ≤ 50 tokens, each argument ≤ 12\n   words). It is then in the CLI, REPL and MCP.\n3. Put enums and op shapes in `schema()`; a new asset kind is a `KIND_ROUTES` row, not a new tool.\n4. Add actions that change what the live studio shows to `live_feedback::changes_studio`.\n5. Add a clause to the `mcp.rs` instructions if agents should find the tool first.\n6. Test it on `Session::new(\"empty\", 1)`.\n\n### Adding a room (preferred: data only, no rebuild)\nCopy `rooms/_template.toml` to `rooms/<key>.toml` and edit; the template documents every field\n(`rooms/feel_lab.toml` and `crates/pav_core/tests/physics_room.toml` are full examples). The room\nis placed on its wing's corridor automatically. Every room is a station of the world demo and\nteaches: give it a `[learn]` block and a `note` on every pad that changes something\n(`pav_view/tests/learn.rs` checks them; `rooms/bloom.toml` is the model). Check it with\n`pav room_check path=rooms/<key>.toml`; look at it with `pav capture scene=<key>` (the room alone,\nfast) or `printf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=<key>\\ncapture\\n' | pav repl`. The running game\nhot-reloads room files. New *mechanics* are Rust (character.rs / sim.rs behaviours / entity\nfields); keep rooms as data wherever possible.\n\n### Scenes\n`world` (pavilion + rooms + streaming wilderness), `world/<room>` (start in a room),\n`<room key>` (that room alone, no terrain), `test`, `empty`, and Shardfall's `town` (the game\nstarts here by default; the saved hero is loaded into any game scene), `arena`, `lab` (the\nMenagerie of creatures) and `level/<n>` (depth n: 1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\nGames built on the hook (`pav_core::games::GAMES`) are scenes by their key: `starter` today.\nCode scenes live in `scenes.rs` (Shardfall places in `arpg/scene.rs`).\n\n## Tests\n`cargo test` runs every suite; `cargo test -p <crate> --test <suite>` runs one. Suites live in\n`crates/*/tests/`, named for what they cover (`docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Tests\", describes each).\n`pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` and `movement.rs` are the cheapest way to catch feel regressions:\nextend them when you add movement features. `pav_view/tests/material_parity.rs` needs a GPU (or\nlavapipe); `pav_tools/tests/import_khronos.rs` is opt-in (`-- --ignored`).\n\n## Conventions\n- Rust stable pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`; `Cargo.lock` committed; edition 2024.\n- Finished work gets a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what changed, how it works,\n  the decisions); then update `docs/PROGRESS.md`, which stays one page of current state.\n- Decide anything that isn't genuinely the user's call; record the decision with its entry in\n  `docs/HISTORY.md`. Escalate what the doctrine says to escalate, and log it in\n  `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n- Finishing a task or milestone: commit, push, open a PR into `main`, merge it and delete the\n  branch. This is standard procedure (the user's standing instruction, which counts as asking for\n  the PR); don't ask first.\n- The user plays the Windows build; the log file `shardfall.log` and `shardfall.toml` (startup\n  settings, e.g. `backend = \"dx12\"`) sit next to the `.exe`.\n- Keep this file short: under 32 KiB (Codex reads no further; `docs_limits.rs` checks it) and\n  about 250 lines. Detail goes to `docs/REFERENCE.md` or a topic doc, with a pointer here.\n\n## Code Review Rules\nFor reviewers (Codex when it runs, or `/code-review`). Flag a change that:\n- breaks one of the \"Key rules\" under Architecture: a graphics, windowing or audio dependency in\n  `pav_core`, play state outside a `Clone` `SimState`, results that can differ between runs on one\n  machine (unseeded randomness, the clock, `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order);\n- commits a binary asset other than a font, or content a licence keeps in the local tiers;\n- makes threads, blocking calls or file access reachable on `wasm32`.\nLeave style, wording and test coverage of routine code alone: this is a one-user prototype\n(\"Working rules\" 5, \"Testing budget\").\n# Shardfall and the Pavilion engine: start here\n\nA Rust game engine built for AI agents (Pavilion), the hack-and-slash built on it (Shardfall)\nand a pavilion of demo rooms. This page is for an agent receiving the source, from a clone of\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall or from a handoff archive (no Git history, build\ncaches, saved heroes or executables; `git init` if you want to track your changes).\n\n## Which route\n- **Make a new game on the engine:** read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It is the only page\n  you need to start; it begins from the starter game (`crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n- **Work on the engine or on Shardfall:** read `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md` (Claude Code loads them through `CLAUDE.md`); `docs/DESIGN.md` and\n  `docs/GAME.md` when the work touches the design.\n\nPrompts to give the receiving agent:\n\n> Make a game: read START_HERE.md and docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md, install the Linux packages and\n> build the `pav` CLI. Then make *(your idea, e.g. \"a top-down maze where you collect five\n> keys and escape while ghosts patrol\")* on the engine, starting from the starter game. Give\n> it a bot that wins and the tests from the guide, check it with the capture tools, tell me\n> how to play it, and end with the engine feedback the guide asks for.\n\n> Work on the engine: read START_HERE.md, then follow AGENTS.md. *(the task)*\n\nAgents in auto modes may refuse to unpack an archive or run its scripts unless the prompt\nsays to; say so in the prompt when you hand over an archive.\n\n## Linux setup\nUse a Linux machine with Rust installed through rustup. `rust-toolchain.toml` selects\nRust 1.98.1 automatically. The first build needs network access to download the\ntoolchain and dependencies; `Cargo.lock` pins the dependency versions.\n\nOn Debian / Ubuntu, install the native build and runtime dependencies below. If\nalready running as root, omit `sudo`. On a managed machine, ask its administrator\nto install missing packages.\n\n```sh\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y build-essential pkg-config libasound2-dev libudev-dev \\\n  mesa-vulkan-drivers vulkan-tools xvfb libxkbcommon-x11-0 libx11-xcb1 \\\n  libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6\n```\n\nSimulation tools need no display or GPU. Screenshot tools use Vulkan and can use\nMesa lavapipe (software rendering) on a machine without a GPU. Keep builds on disk\nand limit parallel jobs on machines with little RAM.\n\n## Build and inspect headlessly\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav\n./target/debug/pav help\n./target/debug/pav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=5\n./target/debug/pav capture scene=world ticks=60 width=640 height=360 out=out/pavilion.png\n```\n\n`starter` is the starter game; `world` is the pavilion of demo rooms; `town` is Shardfall's\ntown; `arena` is the wave arena; `lab` is the Menagerie; `level/1` starts the descent. A room\nkey such as `drift` loads that demo by itself. `pav repl` keeps a session across commands:\n\n```sh\nprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture width=640 height=360 out=out/drift.png\\n' | ./target/debug/pav repl\n./target/debug/pav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\n./target/debug/pav campaign scene=town from=1 to=3 wall=60\ncargo test --locked -p pav_core\n```\n\nInspect the generated images with the agent's image viewer. `docs/REFERENCE.md` describes\nevery tool. `.mcp.json` exposes the same CLI as the `shardfall` MCP server; run the `cargo\nbuild` above before starting an MCP client, because a first build takes longer than clients\nwait for a server.\n\n## Run the game window\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_app\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene starter # the starter game\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene world   # tech demo\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene town    # Shardfall\n```\n\nWithout a graphical desktop, use `xvfb-run -a` around the game command, or use the\nheadless capture tools above. The demo's F2 menu jumps between rooms. Startup\nsettings and hero saves are written next to the game executable.\n\nFor Windows cross-compilation and Wine checks, `scripts/setup-linux.sh` installs the\nadditional tools and `scripts/build-windows.sh` builds the executables.\n\n## The studios (live editing in the window)\n- **Objects:** `scripts/asset-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.studio.json` for the LLM: `assets`,\n  `asset_edit`, `asset_preview`, `asset_spawn` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n  `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`).\n- **Animation:** `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.animation-studio.json` (or `pav\n  live` in a second terminal): `anim_edit`, `anim_preview` (`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`).\n- **Creatures:** Node 22.18 or newer, then `scripts/creature-studio.sh`; `creature_edit`\n  returns a job to follow with `creature_status` (`docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`).\n\nEdits save to readable files and show in the window without a rebuild. `.mcp.json` runs a\nseparate headless session; the studio configurations control the visible window.\n\n## Make another handoff archive\nFrom a Git checkout, `scripts/package-agent.sh` packages the tracked files (with local edits)\ninto `out/agent/shardfall-linux-agent.tar.gz` and a SHA-256 checksum; add new files to Git\nfirst. It leaves out `pavilion-lite/` (an older engine, frozen). `scripts/check-handoff.sh`\nunpacks a fresh archive elsewhere, builds it and plays the starter, as a receiving agent\nwould: run it after changing what the archive carries or this page.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat crates/pav_core/src/games/mod.rs crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.toml && cat crates/pav_core/src/input.rs'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat crates/pav_core/src/games/mod.rs crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.toml && cat crates/pav_core/src/input.rs'","aggregated_output":"//! Games built on the engine: the hook a new game implements, and the list of games.\n//!\n//! A game is a struct holding its rules' state (`Clone` and serde, like everything in\n//! `SimState`) that implements [`Game`], plus a `build` function that makes its scene. One\n//! line in [`GAMES`] and `load scene=<key>` starts it in the tools, the window\n//! (`shardfall --scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`). `starter.rs` is the template\n//! to copy; `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` is the guide. Shardfall (`crate::arpg`) predates this hook and\n//! keeps its own.\n\nuse std::any::Any;\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nuse anyhow::Result;\nuse serde::de::{DeserializeOwned, Error as _};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Deserializer, Serialize, Serializer};\n\nuse crate::entity::EntityId;\nuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\nuse crate::sim::Sim;\n\npub mod starter;\n\n/// Every game the engine knows. `load scene=<key>` (or `<key>/<rest>`) builds it.\npub static GAMES: &[Entry] =\n    &[entry::<starter::Starter>(\"Starter: collect every shard while slimes chase you (the template to copy).\", starter::build)];\n\n/// Still playing, won or lost.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\npub enum Outcome {\n    #[default]\n    Playing,\n    Won,\n    Lost,\n}\n\n/// A game's rules. Every method has a default, so a game implements only what it uses.\n///\n/// The engine calls `pre` and `post` once per tick (60 a second) from `Sim::step`; everything\n/// else is read-only. While `pre` or `post` runs, the game is out of `sim.state.play`, so use\n/// `self` for the game's own state and `sim` for the world.\npub trait Game: Clone + Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send + Sync + 'static {\n    /// The scene key that starts the game: `load scene=<KEY>`, `--scene <KEY>`, `?scene=<KEY>`.\n    const KEY: &'static str;\n\n    /// Before characters move: read the player's input and decide what the characters the\n    /// game drives do this tick. A returned input replaces that character's own (its `ai`\n    /// brain, or the player's keys for the player).\n    fn pre(\n        &mut self,\n        _sim: &mut Sim,\n        _input: &InputFrame,\n        _dt: f32,\n        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        BTreeMap::new()\n    }\n\n    /// After physics: the rules (pickups, damage, score, win and loss). Push `SimEvent`s for\n    /// sounds and effects (`Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`...).\n    fn post(&mut self, _sim: &mut Sim, _dt: f32, _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {}\n\n    /// HUD lines: drawn at the top left of the window, returned by the `play` tool.\n    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        Vec::new()\n    }\n\n    /// The game's state for tools and tests (`play`), as JSON.\n    fn status(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        serde_json::Value::Null\n    }\n\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        Outcome::Playing\n    }\n\n    /// The input a good player would give this tick. `play bot=true` and the tests drive the\n    /// player with it; teach it every new rule, and keep a test that it wins.\n    fn bot(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        InputFrame::default()\n    }\n}\n\n/// One line of [`GAMES`]: made with [`entry`].\npub struct Entry {\n    pub key: &'static str,\n    pub about: &'static str,\n    /// Builds the scene and sets `sim.state.play`. The `&str` is the rest of the scene name\n    /// after `<key>/` (a level number, say), if any.\n    pub build: fn(&mut Sim, Option<&str>) -> Result<()>,\n    load: fn(ciborium::Value) -> Result<GameBox, String>,\n}\n\n/// A [`GAMES`] line for the game type `G`.\npub const fn entry<G: Game>(about: &'static str, build: fn(&mut Sim, Option<&str>) -> Result<()>) -> Entry {\n    Entry { key: G::KEY, about, build, load: load_as::<G> }\n}\n\nfn load_as<G: Game>(v: ciborium::Value) -> Result<GameBox, String> {\n    v.deserialized::<G>().map(GameBox::new).map_err(|e| e.to_string())\n}\n\n/// A game's level text: `crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` read from disk when the program\n/// runs from the repository's root (`pav`, `cargo run`), so an edit shows at the next `load`\n/// with no rebuild; else `built_in`, the copy embedded with `include_str!` (tests, the browser,\n/// a shipped executable).\npub fn level_text(key: &str, built_in: &'static str) -> std::borrow::Cow<'static, str> {\n    #[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\n    if let Ok(text) = std::fs::read_to_string(format!(\"crates/pav_core/src/games/{key}.toml\")) {\n        return text.into();\n    }\n    #[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")]\n    let _ = key; // the browser has no files\n    built_in.into()\n}\n\n/// The game registered under `key`.\npub fn find(key: &str) -> Option<&'static Entry> {\n    GAMES.iter().find(|g| g.key == key)\n}\n\n/// A running game in `SimState::play`: clones (rewind, snapshots) and serializes (snapshot\n/// files, the state hash) through the game's own derives.\npub struct GameBox(Box<dyn DynGame>);\n\nimpl GameBox {\n    pub fn new<G: Game>(game: G) -> Self {\n        Self(Box::new(game))\n    }\n\n    pub fn key(&self) -> &'static str {\n        self.0.key()\n    }\n\n    /// The game as its own type (None for another game).\n    pub fn get<G: Game>(&self) -> Option<&G> {\n        self.0.as_any().downcast_ref()\n    }\n\n    pub fn get_mut<G: Game>(&mut self) -> Option<&mut G> {\n        self.0.as_any_mut().downcast_mut()\n    }\n\n    pub fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        self.0.hud(sim)\n    }\n\n    pub fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        self.0.status(sim)\n    }\n\n    pub fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.0.outcome()\n    }\n\n    pub fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        self.0.bot(sim)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Clone for GameBox {\n    fn clone(&self) -> Self {\n        Self(self.0.clone_box())\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\nstruct Saved {\n    key: String,\n    state: ciborium::Value,\n}\n\nimpl Serialize for GameBox {\n    fn serialize<S: Serializer>(&self, s: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {\n        let state = self.0.save().map_err(serde::ser::Error::custom)?;\n        Saved { key: self.key().to_string(), state }.serialize(s)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<'de> Deserialize<'de> for GameBox {\n    fn deserialize<D: Deserializer<'de>>(d: D) -> Result<Self, D::Error> {\n        let saved = Saved::deserialize(d)?;\n        let Some(entry) = find(&saved.key) else {\n            let known: Vec<&str> = GAMES.iter().map(|g| g.key).collect();\n            return Err(D::Error::custom(format!(\"unknown game '{}' (known: {})\", saved.key, known.join(\", \"))));\n        };\n        (entry.load)(saved.state).map_err(D::Error::custom)\n    }\n}\n\n/// [`Game`] as an object (the trait itself isn't one: `Clone` and serde).\ntrait DynGame: Send + Sync {\n    fn key(&self) -> &'static str;\n    fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn DynGame>;\n    fn save(&self) -> Result<ciborium::Value, String>;\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>);\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value;\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;\n    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any;\n    fn as_any_mut(&mut self) -> &mut dyn Any;\n}\n\nimpl<G: Game> DynGame for G {\n    fn key(&self) -> &'static str {\n        G::KEY\n    }\n    fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn DynGame> {\n        Box::new(self.clone())\n    }\n    fn save(&self) -> Result<ciborium::Value, String> {\n        ciborium::Value::serialized(self).map_err(|e| e.to_string())\n    }\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        Game::pre(self, sim, input, dt, events)\n    }\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        Game::post(self, sim, dt, events)\n    }\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        Game::hud(self, sim)\n    }\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        Game::status(self, sim)\n    }\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        Game::outcome(self)\n    }\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        Game::bot(self, sim)\n    }\n    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any {\n        self\n    }\n    fn as_any_mut(&mut self) -> &mut dyn Any {\n        self\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Sim {\n    /// The running game from [`GAMES`], if this scene is one.\n    pub fn play(&self) -> Option<&GameBox> {\n        self.state.play.as_ref()\n    }\n\n    /// The running game's outcome (`Playing` when there is none).\n    pub fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.play().map(|g| g.outcome()).unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    /// The running game's bot input (none without a game).\n    pub fn bot_input(&self) -> InputFrame {\n        self.play().map(|g| g.bot(self)).unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    pub(crate) fn play_pre(&mut self, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        let Some(mut g) = self.state.play.take() else { return BTreeMap::new() };\n        let out = g.0.pre(self, input, dt, events);\n        self.state.play = Some(g);\n        out\n    }\n\n    pub(crate) fn play_post(&mut self, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        let Some(mut g) = self.state.play.take() else { return };\n        g.0.post(self, dt, events);\n        self.state.play = Some(g);\n    }\n\n    pub(crate) fn play_hud(&self) -> Vec<String> {\n        self.play().map(|g| g.hud(self)).unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    /// Plays the running game with its bot until it is won or lost, or `ticks` pass.\n    /// Returns the outcome and the ticks played.\n    pub fn run_bot(&mut self, ticks: u64) -> (Outcome, u64) {\n        for t in 0..ticks {\n            let o = self.outcome();\n            if o != Outcome::Playing {\n                return (o, t);\n            }\n            let input = self.bot_input();\n            self.step(&input);\n        }\n        (self.outcome(), ticks)\n    }\n}\n//! The starter game, and the template for a new one: collect every shard while slimes chase\n//! you round the walls. Three lives; a slime's touch sends everyone back to the start.\n//!\n//! To start a game of your own, copy this file and `starter.toml` to `<key>.rs` and\n//! `<key>.toml`, change `KEY`, add its line to `GAMES` in `mod.rs`, then change the rules.\n//! `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` walks through it.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\nuse std::sync::Arc;\n\nuse anyhow::{Result, anyhow};\nuse glam::{Vec2, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\nuse serde_json::json;\n\nuse super::{Game, GameBox, Outcome};\nuse crate::color::Color;\nuse crate::entity::{Behavior, EntityId, Spawn};\nuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\nuse crate::nav::NavGrid;\nuse crate::room::RoomDef;\nuse crate::shape::{Shape, Visual};\nuse crate::sim::Sim;\n\n/// The level, a room file, built in (the browser has no files). Running from the repository,\n/// `level_text` reads the file on disk instead, so an edit shows at the next `load`.\nconst LEVEL: &str = include_str!(\"starter.toml\");\n\n/// The rules' numbers: the level's `[game]` table, each with a default, so balancing is an\n/// edit to the level and a `load` (no rebuild).\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(default, deny_unknown_fields)]\npub struct Rules {\n    pub lives: u32,\n    /// Slime speed as a fraction of a walker's top speed (each slime gets ±15%, from the seed).\n    pub slime_speed: f32,\n    /// Slimes chase a player within this walking distance (m).\n    pub slime_sight: f32,\n    /// Feet closer than this (m) catch the player.\n    pub catch: f32,\n    /// Feet closer than this (m) to a shard pick it up.\n    pub pick: f32,\n    /// Seconds the player is safe at the start and after a catch.\n    pub grace: f32,\n}\n\nimpl Default for Rules {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self { lives: 3, slime_speed: 0.5, slime_sight: 9.0, catch: 0.8, pick: 0.9, grace: 2.0 }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct Starter {\n    pub rules: Rules,\n    /// Shards still on the floor.\n    pub shards: Vec<EntityId>,\n    pub total: usize,\n    pub slimes: Vec<EntityId>,\n    /// Each slime's start (feet) and speed.\n    pub homes: Vec<Vec3>,\n    pub speeds: Vec<f32>,\n    pub lives: u32,\n    /// Seconds the player is still safe from slimes.\n    pub grace: f32,\n    pub time: f32,\n    pub outcome: Outcome,\n    /// The walkable grid. It follows from the level alone, so it isn't saved: rebuilt when\n    /// missing (after a snapshot load).\n    #[serde(skip)]\n    nav: Option<Arc<NavGrid>>,\n}\n\n/// Builds the level, places the shards on its `shard` markers and starts the game.\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, _rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()> {\n    let def = RoomDef::parse(&super::level_text(Starter::KEY, LEVEL)).map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml: {e}\"))?;\n    let rules: Rules = toml::Value::Table(def.game.clone()).try_into().map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml [game]: {e}\"))?;\n    crate::scenes::build_standalone_room(sim, Starter::KEY, def);\n    let mut shards = Vec::new();\n    for at in crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\") {\n        let mut look = Visual::new(Shape::Box { half: Vec3::splat(0.2) }, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"));\n        look.emissive = 0.8;\n        let shard = Spawn::new(\"shard\", at + Vec3::Y * 0.7).visual(look).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed: 2.5 });\n        shards.push(sim.spawn(shard));\n    }\n    // Slimes come from the level's [[npc]] entries; the game drives them.\n    let slimes: Vec<EntityId> = sim.state.entities.iter().filter(|e| e.name == \"slime\").map(|e| e.id).collect();\n    let homes = slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n    // Randomness comes from the sim's seeded generator, so a seed always plays the same.\n    let speeds = slimes.iter().map(|_| rules.slime_speed * sim.state.rng.range(0.85, 1.15)).collect();\n    let game = Starter {\n        total: shards.len(),\n        shards,\n        slimes,\n        homes,\n        speeds,\n        lives: rules.lives,\n        grace: rules.grace,\n        rules,\n        time: 0.0,\n        outcome: Outcome::Playing,\n        nav: None,\n    };\n    sim.state.play = Some(GameBox::new(game));\n    Ok(())\n}\n\nimpl Game for Starter {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"starter\";\n\n    fn pre(\n        &mut self,\n        sim: &mut Sim,\n        _input: &InputFrame,\n        _dt: f32,\n        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        let mut out = BTreeMap::new();\n        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n            // Decided: everyone stands still.\n            for &id in sim.state.player.iter().chain(&self.slimes) {\n                out.insert(id, InputFrame::default());\n            }\n            return out;\n        }\n        if self.nav.is_none() {\n            self.nav = NavGrid::for_room(sim).map(Arc::new);\n        }\n        let Some(nav) = self.nav.clone() else { return out };\n        // One flow field toward the player steers every slime round the walls.\n        let player = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id));\n        let field = player.filter(|_| self.grace <= 0.0).and_then(|p| nav.flow(p, self.rules.slime_sight));\n        for (&id, &speed) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.speeds) {\n            let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n            if let (Some(field), Some(at)) = (&field, sim.feet(id))\n                && let Some(d) = field.dir(&nav, at)\n            {\n                input.move_dir = Vec2::new(d.x, d.z) * speed;\n            }\n            out.insert(id, input);\n        }\n        out\n    }\n\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n            return;\n        }\n        self.time += dt;\n        self.grace = (self.grace - dt).max(0.0);\n        let Some(pid) = sim.state.player else { return };\n        let Some(p) = sim.feet(pid) else { return };\n\n        // Shards: walk over one to pick it up.\n        let near: Vec<(EntityId, Vec3)> = self\n            .shards\n            .iter()\n            .filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| (id, e.pos)))\n            .filter(|(_, at)| flat(*at - p) < self.rules.pick)\n            .collect();\n        for (id, at) in near {\n            self.shards.retain(|&s| s != id);\n            sim.despawn(id);\n            events.push(SimEvent::Coin { pos: at });\n            let left = self.shards.len();\n            sim.say(if left == 1 { \"1 shard to go\".to_string() } else { format!(\"{left} shards to go\") });\n        }\n        if self.shards.is_empty() {\n            self.outcome = Outcome::Won;\n            sim.say(format!(\"All shards in {:.1} s!\", self.time));\n            return;\n        }\n\n        // Slimes: a touch costs a life and sends everyone back to the start.\n        let catch = self.rules.catch;\n        let caught = self.grace <= 0.0 && self.slimes.iter().any(|&id| sim.feet(id).is_some_and(|s| flat(s - p) < catch));\n        if caught {\n            self.lives -= 1;\n            events.push(SimEvent::Hit { pos: p + Vec3::Y, strength: 1.0, target: Some(pid) });\n            if self.lives == 0 {\n                self.outcome = Outcome::Lost;\n                sim.say(\"Caught! Game over\");\n                return;\n            }\n            sim.say(format!(\"Caught! {} lives left\", self.lives));\n            let spawn = sim.state.spawn;\n            sim.set_position(pid, spawn);\n            for (&id, &home) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.homes) {\n                sim.set_position(id, home);\n            }\n            self.grace = self.rules.grace;\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        let mut lines =\n            vec![format!(\"Shards {}/{}\", self.total - self.shards.len(), self.total), format!(\"Lives {}\", self.lives)];\n        match self.outcome {\n            Outcome::Won => lines.push(format!(\"You win! {:.1} s\", self.time)),\n            Outcome::Lost => lines.push(\"Game over\".into()),\n            Outcome::Playing => {}\n        }\n        lines\n    }\n\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        let xz = |id: EntityId| sim.feet(id).map(|f| [round(f.x), round(f.z)]);\n        json!({\n            \"outcome\": self.outcome,\n            \"time\": round(self.time),\n            \"shards_left\": self.shards.len(),\n            \"total\": self.total,\n            \"lives\": self.lives,\n            \"grace\": round(self.grace),\n            \"player\": sim.state.player.and_then(xz),\n            \"shards\": self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| [round(e.pos.x), round(e.pos.z)])).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n            \"slimes\": self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| xz(id)).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n        })\n    }\n\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.outcome\n    }\n\n    /// Takes the shortest walk to a shard that keeps clear of slimes (cells near them count\n    /// as walls); with every shard cut off, makes for the reachable spot farthest from them.\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n        let Some(p) = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id)) else { return input };\n        let Some(mut grid) = self.nav.as_deref().cloned().or_else(|| NavGrid::for_room(sim)) else { return input };\n        let slimes: Vec<Vec3> = self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n        if self.grace < 0.5 {\n            for i in 0..grid.blocked.len() {\n                if slimes.iter().any(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s) < 1.8) {\n                    grid.blocked[i] = true;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        let paths = self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id)).filter_map(|e| grid.path(p, e.pos));\n        let path = paths.min_by(|a, b| length(p, a).total_cmp(&length(p, b))).or_else(|| {\n            let clearance = |i: usize| slimes.iter().map(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s)).fold(f32::MAX, f32::min);\n            let reach = grid.flow(p, 6.0)?;\n            let safest = (0..grid.blocked.len())\n                .filter(|&i| reach.dist[i].is_finite())\n                .max_by(|&a, &b| clearance(a).total_cmp(&clearance(b)))?;\n            grid.path(p, grid.center(safest))\n        });\n        input.move_dir = path.map(|path| flat_dir(path[0] - p)).unwrap_or_default();\n        input\n    }\n}\n\nfn flat(v: Vec3) -> f32 {\n    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).length()\n}\n\nfn flat_dir(v: Vec3) -> Vec2 {\n    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).normalize_or_zero()\n}\n\n/// Walking length of a path from `p`.\nfn length(p: Vec3, path: &[Vec3]) -> f32 {\n    let (mut at, mut sum) = (p, 0.0);\n    for &q in path {\n        sum += flat(q - at);\n        at = q;\n    }\n    sum\n}\n\nfn round(x: f32) -> f64 {\n    (x as f64 * 100.0).round() / 100.0\n}\n# The starter game's level: a room file (every field: rooms/_template.toml). Column = x (east),\n# row = z (south), 1 cell = 1 m. `o` cells are shards and `P` is the start (legend markers,\n# read by starter.rs with `scenes::markers`); the slimes are [[npc]] entries the game drives.\n# Check it with `pav room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.toml`.\n\nname = \"Starter\"\nabout = \"Collect every shard while slimes chase you.\"\nentrance = { at = [8, 12], facing = \"south\" }   # required by the format; the `P` marker is the start\n\n# The rules' numbers (starter.rs `Rules`). Running from the repository, `pav` reads this file at\n# each `load`: edit, then `load scene=starter` or `play bot=true seeds=10`, no rebuild.\n[game]\nlives = 3\nslime_speed = 0.5\nslime_sight = 9.0\ncatch = 0.8\npick = 0.9\ngrace = 2.0\n\n[camera]\ntilt = 58.0\ndistance = 17.0\n\n[layout]\norigin = [-9.0, 0.0, -6.5]\n\n[[layout.layer]]\ny = 0\nmap = '''\n##################\n#o......#.......o#\n#.......#........#\n#..##.......##...#\n#..#.........#...#\n#.......o........#\n#....#.....#.....#\n#.......P........#\n#..#.........#...#\n#..##.......##...#\n#.......#........#\n#o......#.......o#\n##################\n'''\n\n[layout.legend.'.']\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.5, y1 = 0, color = \"#3b4a5c\" }]\n\n[layout.legend.'#']\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.5, y1 = 0, color = \"#3b4a5c\" }, { y0 = 0, y1 = 1.2, color = \"#8a6f9e\", rounded = true }]\n\n[layout.legend.'o']\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.5, y1 = 0, color = \"#3b4a5c\" }]\nmarker = \"shard\"\n\n[layout.legend.'P']\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.5, y1 = 0, color = \"#3b4a5c\" }]\nmarker = \"player\"\n\n[[npc]]\nname = \"slime\"\nkind = \"blob\"\npos = [12.5, 0, 2.5]\nlook = { skin = \"#6cd66c\" }\n\n[[npc]]\nname = \"slime\"\nkind = \"blob\"\npos = [5.5, 0, 10.5]\nlook = { skin = \"#6cd66c\" }\n\n[[npc]]\nname = \"slime\"\nkind = \"blob\"\npos = [15.5, 0, 7.5]\nlook = { skin = \"#6cd66c\" }\n//! Device-independent input for one simulation tick. The host (window, agent, replay) fills\n//! it; the simulation never sees devices. Directions are already in world space.\n\nuse glam::{Vec2, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n\npub mod buttons {\n    pub const JUMP: u32 = 1 << 0;\n    pub const CROUCH: u32 = 1 << 1;\n    pub const INTERACT: u32 = 1 << 2;\n    pub const USE: u32 = 1 << 3;\n    pub const FOCUS: u32 = 1 << 4;\n    pub const SPRINT: u32 = 1 << 5;\n    pub const PRIMARY: u32 = 1 << 6;\n    pub const SECONDARY: u32 = 1 << 7;\n    pub const CRAWL: u32 = 1 << 8;\n    /// Shardfall: skill slots 3-6 (slots 1-2 are PRIMARY / SECONDARY), dodge and potion.\n    pub const SKILL3: u32 = 1 << 9;\n    pub const SKILL4: u32 = 1 << 10;\n    pub const SKILL5: u32 = 1 << 11;\n    pub const SKILL6: u32 = 1 << 12;\n    pub const DODGE: u32 = 1 << 13;\n    pub const POTION: u32 = 1 << 14;\n\n    pub const NAMES: &[(&str, u32)] = &[\n        (\"jump\", JUMP),\n        (\"crouch\", CROUCH),\n        (\"interact\", INTERACT),\n        (\"use\", USE),\n        (\"focus\", FOCUS),\n        (\"sprint\", SPRINT),\n        (\"primary\", PRIMARY),\n        (\"secondary\", SECONDARY),\n        (\"crawl\", CRAWL),\n        (\"skill3\", SKILL3),\n        (\"skill4\", SKILL4),\n        (\"skill5\", SKILL5),\n        (\"skill6\", SKILL6),\n        (\"dodge\", DODGE),\n        (\"potion\", POTION),\n    ];\n\n    pub fn from_name(n: &str) -> Option<u32> {\n        NAMES.iter().find(|(k, _)| k.eq_ignore_ascii_case(n)).map(|(_, v)| *v)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct InputFrame {\n    /// Desired movement on the ground plane: x = world +X, y = world +Z. Length <= 1.\n    pub move_dir: Vec2,\n    /// Vertical intent for climbing/flying/swimming (-1..1).\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub vertical: f32,\n    /// World-space aim point (mouse on the ground plane, or stick projection).\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub aim: Option<Vec3>,\n    /// Buttons held this tick.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub held: u32,\n    /// Buttons that went down since the previous tick (never lost, even for short taps).\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub pressed: u32,\n    /// A menu action this tick (Shardfall: equip, sell, travel...), queued one per tick.\n    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]\n    pub cmd: Option<crate::arpg::GameCmd>,\n}\n\nimpl InputFrame {\n    pub fn down(&self, b: u32) -> bool {\n        self.held & b != 0\n    }\n    pub fn just(&self, b: u32) -> bool {\n        self.pressed & b != 0\n    }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat crates/pav_view/src/camera.rs && sed -n '1,210p' crates/pav_core/src/frame.rs && rg -n 'fn build|frame.game|frame.play|camera|cutaway' crates/pav_view/src/lib.rs\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat crates/pav_view/src/camera.rs && sed -n '1,210p' crates/pav_core/src/frame.rs && rg -n 'fn build|frame.game|frame.play|camera|cutaway' crates/pav_view/src/lib.rs\"","aggregated_output":"//! The camera rig: every parameter is live. \"2D top-down\" is just tilt 90 + orthographic.\n\nuse glam::{Mat4, Vec2, Vec3};\nuse pav_core::params::{ParamVisitor, Tunable};\nuse pav_render::CameraData;\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(default)]\npub struct CameraParams {\n    /// Degrees above the horizon: 90 = straight down, 0 = side view.\n    pub tilt: f32,\n    /// Degrees; 0 = looking north (-Z).\n    pub yaw: f32,\n    /// Distance from the focus point (metres). In orthographic mode it sets the zoom.\n    pub distance: f32,\n    /// Vertical field of view (degrees). Orthographic uses it to match framing.\n    pub fov: f32,\n    pub ortho: bool,\n    /// Seconds for the camera to catch up with the focus (0 = locked).\n    pub follow_lag: f32,\n    /// Look slightly above the focus point (metres).\n    pub height_offset: f32,\n}\n\nimpl Default for CameraParams {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self { tilt: 62.0, yaw: 0.0, distance: 18.0, fov: 40.0, ortho: false, follow_lag: 0.12, height_offset: 0.8 }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Tunable for CameraParams {\n    fn visit(&mut self, v: &mut dyn ParamVisitor) {\n        v.float(\"tilt\", &mut self.tilt, 0.0, 90.0, \"Degrees above the horizon (90 = top-down, 0 = side view)\");\n        v.float(\"yaw\", &mut self.yaw, -180.0, 180.0, \"Camera rotation around the focus (degrees)\");\n        v.float(\"distance\", &mut self.distance, 2.0, 120.0, \"Distance / zoom (m)\");\n        v.float(\"fov\", &mut self.fov, 5.0, 100.0, \"Vertical field of view (degrees)\");\n        v.bool(\"ortho\", &mut self.ortho, \"Orthographic projection (flat 2D look)\");\n        v.float(\"follow_lag\", &mut self.follow_lag, 0.0, 1.0, \"Seconds to catch up with the target\");\n        v.float(\"height_offset\", &mut self.height_offset, -2.0, 4.0, \"Aim above the target (m)\");\n    }\n}\n\nimpl CameraParams {\n    pub const PRESETS: &'static [(&'static str, fn() -> CameraParams)] = &[\n        (\"classic 62°\", || CameraParams::default()),\n        (\"top-down 90° ortho\", || CameraParams { tilt: 90.0, ortho: true, distance: 22.0, ..Default::default() }),\n        (\"high 80°\", || CameraParams { tilt: 80.0, ..Default::default() }),\n        (\"70°\", || CameraParams { tilt: 70.0, ..Default::default() }),\n        (\"45°\", || CameraParams { tilt: 45.0, distance: 16.0, ..Default::default() }),\n        (\"isometric\", || CameraParams { tilt: 35.264, yaw: 45.0, ortho: true, distance: 24.0, ..Default::default() }),\n        (\"side view\", || CameraParams { tilt: 0.0, ortho: true, distance: 20.0, height_offset: 2.0, ..Default::default() }),\n        (\"close 3rd person\", || CameraParams { tilt: 25.0, distance: 7.0, fov: 60.0, height_offset: 1.4, ..Default::default() }),\n    ];\n}\n\nimpl CameraParams {\n    /// Blend between two camera setups (yaw takes the short way round; projection switches\n    /// halfway).\n    pub fn lerp(&self, o: &CameraParams, t: f32) -> CameraParams {\n        let l = |a: f32, b: f32| a + (b - a) * t;\n        let dyaw = ((o.yaw - self.yaw + 540.0).rem_euclid(360.0)) - 180.0;\n        CameraParams {\n            tilt: l(self.tilt, o.tilt),\n            yaw: ((self.yaw + dyaw * t + 540.0).rem_euclid(360.0)) - 180.0,\n            distance: l(self.distance, o.distance),\n            fov: l(self.fov, o.fov),\n            ortho: if t < 0.5 { self.ortho } else { o.ortho },\n            follow_lag: l(self.follow_lag, o.follow_lag),\n            height_offset: l(self.height_offset, o.height_offset),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub struct CameraRig {\n    /// Target parameters (what the panel edits).\n    pub params: CameraParams,\n    /// Smoothed focus point.\n    pub target: Vec3,\n    initialized: bool,\n    /// Blending from earlier parameters: (from, elapsed, duration).\n    blend: Option<(CameraParams, f32, f32)>,\n}\n\nimpl Default for CameraRig {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self::new(CameraParams::default())\n    }\n}\n\nimpl CameraRig {\n    pub fn new(params: CameraParams) -> Self {\n        Self { params, target: Vec3::ZERO, initialized: false, blend: None }\n    }\n\n    /// Parameters in effect right now (blended while a transition runs).\n    pub fn current(&self) -> CameraParams {\n        match &self.blend {\n            Some((from, e, d)) => {\n                let t = (e / d.max(1e-3)).clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n                from.lerp(&self.params, t * t * (3.0 - 2.0 * t))\n            }\n            None => self.params.clone(),\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Call before changing `params` to glide there over `seconds` instead of cutting.\n    pub fn blend_from_current(&mut self, seconds: f32) {\n        self.blend = Some((self.current(), 0.0, seconds));\n    }\n\n    /// Follows `focus` with exponential smoothing.\n    pub fn update(&mut self, focus: Vec3, dt: f32) {\n        if let Some(b) = &mut self.blend {\n            b.1 += dt;\n            if b.1 >= b.2 {\n                self.blend = None;\n            }\n        }\n        if !self.initialized || self.params.follow_lag <= 0.0 {\n            self.target = focus;\n            self.initialized = true;\n            return;\n        }\n        let k = 1.0 - (-dt / self.current().follow_lag.max(1e-3)).exp();\n        self.target += (focus - self.target) * k;\n    }\n\n    pub fn snap(&mut self, focus: Vec3) {\n        self.target = focus;\n        self.initialized = true;\n    }\n\n    /// Horizontal forward (camera looking direction flattened), and right.\n    pub fn ground_axes(&self) -> (Vec3, Vec3) {\n        let yaw = self.current().yaw.to_radians();\n        let fwd = Vec3::new(yaw.sin(), 0.0, -yaw.cos());\n        let right = Vec3::new(yaw.cos(), 0.0, yaw.sin());\n        (fwd, right)\n    }\n\n    pub fn forward(&self) -> Vec3 {\n        let (fh, _) = self.ground_axes();\n        let t = self.current().tilt.clamp(0.0, 90.0).to_radians();\n        (fh * t.cos() + Vec3::NEG_Y * t.sin()).normalize()\n    }\n\n    pub fn up(&self) -> Vec3 {\n        let (fh, _) = self.ground_axes();\n        let t = self.current().tilt.clamp(0.0, 90.0).to_radians();\n        (fh * t.sin() + Vec3::Y * t.cos()).normalize()\n    }\n\n    pub fn look_at(&self) -> Vec3 {\n        self.target + Vec3::Y * self.current().height_offset\n    }\n\n    pub fn eye(&self) -> Vec3 {\n        self.look_at() - self.forward() * self.current().distance\n    }\n\n    pub fn data(&self, aspect: f32) -> CameraData {\n        let p = &self.current();\n        let fwd = self.forward();\n        let eye = self.eye();\n        let view = glam::camera::rh::view::look_to_mat4(eye, fwd, self.up());\n        let mut fov = p.fov.clamp(1.0, 170.0).to_radians();\n        if aspect < 1.0 && aspect > 0.0 {\n            // Taller than wide (a phone held upright): the field of view is the width's, so the\n            // sides are not cut down to a sliver.\n            fov = (2.0 * ((fov * 0.5).tan() / aspect).atan()).min(150f32.to_radians());\n        }\n        let far = (p.distance * 4.0 + 200.0).max(300.0);\n        let proj = if p.ortho {\n            let half_h = p.distance * (fov * 0.5).tan();\n            let half_w = half_h * aspect;\n            glam::camera::rh::proj::directx::orthographic(-half_w, half_w, -half_h, half_h, 0.05, far)\n        } else {\n            glam::camera::rh::proj::directx::perspective(fov, aspect, 0.1, far)\n        };\n        CameraData { view, proj, eye, forward: fwd, ortho: p.ortho }\n    }\n\n    /// World-space ray through a pixel (origin, direction).\n    pub fn screen_ray(&self, px: Vec2, size: Vec2) -> (Vec3, Vec3) {\n        let cam = self.data(size.x / size.y.max(1.0));\n        let inv = (cam.proj * cam.view).inverse();\n        let ndc = Vec2::new(px.x / size.x * 2.0 - 1.0, 1.0 - px.y / size.y * 2.0);\n        let near = inv.project_point3(ndc.extend(0.0));\n        let far = inv.project_point3(ndc.extend(1.0));\n        (near, (far - near).normalize())\n    }\n\n    /// Where a pixel's ray hits the horizontal plane at `height`.\n    pub fn ground_point(&self, px: Vec2, size: Vec2, height: f32) -> Option<Vec3> {\n        let (o, d) = self.screen_ray(px, size);\n        if d.y.abs() < 1e-5 {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let t = (height - o.y) / d.y;\n        (t > 0.0).then(|| o + d * t)\n    }\n\n    /// Converts stick/WASD input (x right, y up) into a world direction on the ground plane.\n    pub fn relative_move(&self, input: Vec2) -> Vec2 {\n        let (f, r) = self.ground_axes();\n        let w = r * input.x + f * input.y;\n        Vec2::new(w.x, w.z)\n    }\n\n    pub fn view_proj(&self, aspect: f32) -> Mat4 {\n        let d = self.data(aspect);\n        d.proj * d.view\n    }\n}\n//! What the simulation publishes for rendering each tick. Cheap to clone (static geometry is\n//! shared through `Arc`s).\n\nuse glam::{Quat, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n\nuse crate::entity::EntityId;\nuse crate::puppet::{PuppetDef, PuppetState};\nuse crate::shape::Visual;\nuse crate::statics::StaticWorld;\n\n/// Animation state of a character, drawn as a puppet.\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub struct PuppetFrame {\n    pub state: PuppetState,\n    /// Distance from the entity position (capsule centre) down to the feet.\n    pub feet_offset: f32,\n    /// Own look (NPCs); None = `RenderFrame::puppet_def`.\n    pub def: Option<std::sync::Arc<PuppetDef>>,\n    /// Creature feet and swinging chains.\n    pub rig: Option<crate::rig::RigView>,\n    /// Colour wash (hit flash, frozen, burning...) and its strength.\n    pub tint: Option<([f32; 3], f32)>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub struct RenderObject {\n    pub id: EntityId,\n    pub pos: Vec3,\n    pub rot: Quat,\n    pub visual: Visual,\n    /// Immutable parts belonging to this one object ID.\n    pub prop: Option<crate::prop_instance::PropFrame>,\n    pub puppet: Option<PuppetFrame>,\n    /// Blink/highlight driver: bomb fuse seconds left, or < 0 for none.\n    pub pulse: f32,\n    /// Deformable body: particle positions and how to draw them.\n    pub soft: Option<SoftView>,\n    /// Car / helicopter parts (wheels, rotors).\n    pub vehicle: Option<crate::vehicle::VehicleView>,\n    /// A guard's vision cone.\n    pub cone: Option<crate::stealth::ConeView>,\n    /// Fixed scenery (no character, never moves, not a game actor): view filters treat it\n    /// as part of the environment rather than the characters and objects.\n    pub scenery: bool,\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub struct SoftView {\n    pub points: Vec<Vec3>,\n    /// Surface triangles (cubes, balls, cloth); empty for ropes.\n    pub surface: std::sync::Arc<Vec<[u32; 3]>>,\n    /// Rope segments.\n    pub segments: std::sync::Arc<Vec<[u32; 2]>>,\n    /// Rope thickness.\n    pub radius: f32,\n    /// Sheets are seen from both sides.\n    pub two_sided: bool,\n}\n\n/// Things that happened during a tick, for sound and effects.\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, Serialize, Deserialize, PartialEq)]\n#[serde(tag = \"type\", rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\npub enum SimEvent {\n    Spawned {\n        id: EntityId,\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Impact {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        strength: f32,\n    },\n    Jump {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Land {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        speed: f32,\n    },\n    Explosion {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        radius: f32,\n    },\n    Step {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Throw {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    EnterRoom {\n        room: u16,\n    },\n    ExitRoom {\n        room: u16,\n    },\n    Hit {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        strength: f32,\n        /// The character struck, when it is one.\n        #[serde(default)]\n        target: Option<EntityId>,\n    },\n    Respawn {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    CourseStart {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    CourseFinish {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        time: f32,\n        new_best: bool,\n    },\n    Gate {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        ok: bool,\n    },\n    Checkpoint {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Splash {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Pad {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    /// A blaster shot, an enemy hit, an enemy destroyed.\n    Shot {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Damage {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Destroyed {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        size: f32,\n    },\n    /// A guard raised the alarm.\n    Spotted {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Grab {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Roll {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    /// A tile starts crumbling / breaks.\n    Crack {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Break {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Bounce {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    /// Shardfall: a weapon swing, a hit landing (power = damage, element index), a kill, a\n    /// slam, a spell cast, an explosion, gold, a potion, a block, a level-up.\n    Swing {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        heavy: bool,\n    },\n    Strike {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        power: f32,\n        element: u8,\n        crit: bool,\n    },\n    Slain {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        size: f32,\n    },\n    Slam {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        radius: f32,\n    },\n    Spell {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        element: u8,\n    },\n    Blast {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        element: u8,\n    },\n    Coin {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Potion {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    Block {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    LevelUp {\n        pos: Vec3,\n    },\n    /// An item dropped (rarity 0 normal .. 3 unique) or was picked up.\n    Loot {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        rarity: u8,\n    },\n    Pickup {\n        pos: Vec3,\n        rarity: u8,\n    },\n    /// Arrived somewhere else (Shardfall place code).\n    Travel {\n        place: u32,\n    },\n1://! Turns simulation frames into render scenes: camera rig, interpolation, style settings.\n6:pub mod camera;\n15:pub use camera::{CameraParams, CameraRig};\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
